#. heading 3
#: site/lectures/12-oop-nomenclatures.md:1113
msgid "\"A banded nomenclature is a nomenclature\""
msgstr ""

#. paragraph
#: site/lectures/12-oop-nomenclatures.md:1115
msgid "Some nomenclatures do not map **codes** but **ranges**: days past due to a bucket, a PD to a grade, an amount to a size class. In SAS, a range format:"
msgstr ""

#. paragraph
#: site/lectures/12-oop-nomenclatures.md:1124
msgid "Everything else stays the same: a name, metadata, codes with labels, `apply`, `validate`. Only `lookup` works differently. That is what **inheritance** is for: a **subclass** takes everything of its **base class**, and changes or adds only what is different:"
msgstr ""

#. paragraph
#: site/lectures/12-oop-nomenclatures.md:1171
msgid "`class BandedNomenclature(Nomenclature)` - the base class in brackets. The subclass **is a** `Nomenclature`: it has all its fields and methods."
msgstr ""

#. paragraph
#: site/lectures/12-oop-nomenclatures.md:1173
msgid "`lookup` is **overridden**: the subclass has its own version, and every call of `lookup` - also the one **inside** `apply` and `__contains__`, written in the base class - uses it. This is the power of inheritance."
msgstr ""

#. paragraph
#: site/lectures/12-oop-nomenclatures.md:1176
msgid "`super().__post_init__()` runs the checks of the base class first, then the new checks. Without it, the base checks would be lost."
msgstr ""

#. paragraph
#: site/lectures/12-oop-nomenclatures.md:1179
msgid "The checks of the subclass find a gap between two bands - a value in the gap would get no bucket:"
msgstr ""

#. paragraph
#: site/lectures/12-oop-nomenclatures.md:1208
msgid "Python looks for a method first in the class of the object, then in its base classes, in an order called the **MRO** (method resolution order):"
msgstr ""

#. paragraph
#: site/lectures/12-oop-nomenclatures.md:1231
msgid "`lookup` is found in `BandedNomenclature` itself; `apply` is not there, so Python takes it from `Nomenclature`. (`object` is the base of every class in Python.)"
msgstr ""

#. paragraph
#: site/lectures/12-oop-nomenclatures.md:1234
msgid "`apply` was never written for bands - it comes from the base class and works:"
msgstr ""

#. heading 3
#: site/lectures/12-oop-nomenclatures.md:1267
msgid "The rating scale as bands of PD"
msgstr ""

#. paragraph
#: site/lectures/12-oop-nomenclatures.md:1269
